<TopicPage slug="subjects/earth-science/intermediate/oceans-and-currents">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    The ocean is <strong>the planet\'s heat and carbon flywheel</strong>: wind-driven surface
    gyres redistribute heat in years; the density-driven deep circulation does it in centuries.
    Everything climate-related — weather, marine life, sea level — runs on ocean physics.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="heat budget, gyres, the conveyor, ENSO" widget="equationStep" config={oceanCfg} />

  <h2>Why ocean science is climate science</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>AMOC is the watched system:</strong> the Atlantic branch of the conveyor warms Europe; freshwater melt weakens it. A collapse this century is unlikely but not excluded (IPCC) — the tipping-point lesson\'s most-discussed case.</li>
    <li><strong>Ocean acidification is the quiet crisis:</strong> absorbed CO₂ forms carbonic acid — pH down ~0.1 already (30% more acidity), dissolving shell-building organisms. Climate change\'s twin, less famous, equally measured (the chemistry of the carbonate lesson running in reverse).</li>
    <li><strong>Sea level is layered:</strong> thermal expansion + ice melt + land water — ~20 cm since 1900, accelerating (the coastal geography lesson\'s stakes). Satellite altimetry measures it to millimetres.</li>
    <li><strong>The map is still blank:</strong> ~80% of the ocean floor unmapped at modern resolution; Argo floats (3,800+ robots) keep profiles flowing — oceanography runs on autonomous sensors now (the remote-sensing lesson\'s aquatic wing).</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Broecker, "The Great Ocean Conveyor" (1991); IPCC AR6 Oceans chapter; Argo programme; NOAA ENSO monitoring.</p>
</TopicPage>
